[privilege_escalation]
#become=True
#become_method=sudo
#become_user=root
#become_ask_pass=False

Here's what each line means:

become=True: This would tell Ansible to use privilege escalation (if it wasn't commented out). In other words, it would perform operations with superuser privileges.

become_method=sudo: This would set the method for privilege escalation to sudo (if it wasn't commented out). This is the default method on most systems.

become_user=root: This would tell Ansible to become the root user when escalating privileges (if it wasn't commented out).

become_ask_pass=False: This would tell Ansible not to prompt for a password when escalating privileges (if it wasn't commented out). This is useful if you're using SSH keys for authentication, or if the user Ansible is running as has passwordless sudo enabled.


handler: ==> It is used to restart the service after the changes are made.
  - name: restart apache
    service:
      name: apache2
      state: restarted
  - name: restart mysql
    service:
      name: mysql
      state: restarted

notify:
  - restart apache
  - restart mysql

meta:flush_handlers
--force-handler ==> in case of failure, it will force the handler to run.

Environment Variables: 
  - name: Set environment variables
    environment:
      http_proxy: http://proxy.example.com:8080
      https_proxy: http://proxy.example.com:8080
      no_proxy: localhost,


register: ==> It is used to store the output of the command in a variable.
  - name: Run a command and register the output
    command: /usr/bin/uptime
    register: result

debug: ==> It is used to print the output of the command.
  - name: Debug output
    debug:
      var: result


vars:  ==> It is used to set the variables.
  - name: Set variables
    vars:
      myvar1: value1
      myvar2: value2

when:  ==> It is used to execute the command based on the condition.
  - name: Conditional execution
    command: /usr/bin/uptime
    register: result
    when: result.stdout.find('load') == -1

include_vars:  ==> It is used to include the variables from the file.
  - name: Include variables from a file
    include_vars: "{{ item }}"
    with_first_found:
      - files:
          - /etc/myvars.yml
          - /etc/myvars.yaml
          - /etc/myvars.json
      - missing_ok: yes

